{"agent":"lane-sl-throughput","data":{"budget":"60 min, one suite run, no GPU","claim":"Longest-first ordering with work stealing cuts the p90 wall of a full suite run by more than run-to-run noise, because the slowest test sets the tail.","falsifier":"Wall time unchanged within noise: a scheduler simulation on measured per-test durations shows natural order already within a few percent of the sum-over-workers bound, so no order can save more than that.","item":"PS-suite-wall-tail","mechanism":"xdist hands out tests in collection order; a long file that starts last leaves workers idle at the end.","probe":"One measured full run (ci run --command 'pytest tests -n 24 --dist worksteal --junitxml=...', 22002 tests, wall 1656 s, host load about 110) replayed through a scheduler simulation: load order vs worksteal, natural vs test-LPT vs file-LPT, n=24 and n=48.","probe_output":"sim vs actual at n=24: natural/worksteal 1620.6 s vs measured 1656 s (model within 2.5 percent). Sum/N bound: 1518 s (n=24), 759 s (n=48); longest test 170.7 s, longest file 775.5 s. n=24: natural 1616 (load) / 1621 (worksteal); LPT load 1518 (-6.0 percent); LPT worksteal 1609 to 1664 (no gain, test-LPT worse). n=48: natural 865 / 873; LPT load 759 (-12 percent, the bound); LPT worksteal 858 to 927 (-1 percent to +6 percent). No per-file durations were recorded in the event log or the perf ring before this run, so a p90 over the last 20 runs is not available.","question":"Does starting the longest test files first (longest-processing-time order) cut the wall time of a full run?","sources":[".ddflow/local scratch junit of one measured run (not committed)"],"verdict":"REFUTED"},"id":"e4d8fb8c1aa36309136cdfb37","kind":"research.recorded","lamport":184495,"schema":1,"subject":"Rc32725c0ba","ts":"2026-11-12T15:06:48.313408Z"}
