{"agent":"lane-sl-throughput","data":{"budget":"reused the PS-suite-wall-tail measurement","claim":"A full suite started while other suites already load the host runs many times longer than on an idle host, so holding it until the load falls costs less than running it into the load.","falsifier":"A full run at host load per core near the ceiling takes about as long as the same run on an idle host.","item":"SL-suite-load-budget","mechanism":"Workers contend for cores with the other suites' workers; per-test time inflates, and a per-test timeout can fire.","probe":"ci run --command 'pytest tests -n 24 --dist worksteal --junitxml=...' measured at host load average 107 to 116 (192 cores, 0.55-0.6 per core), 22002 tests; compare with the same suite's idle-host figure recorded by the team (about 24 min serial, about 48 s at -n 48)","probe_output":"measured: wall 1656 s at -n 24, summed per-test time 36442 s (longest test 170.7 s); idle-host reference: serial about 1440 s, so per-test time ran about 25 times its idle cost at load 0.55-0.6 per core; the 170 s longest test sits against a 420 s per-test timeout.","question":"Does host load from concurrent suites stretch a suite run enough that a load budget at admission pays?","sources":["local junit of one measured run (not committed); memory note run-tests-in-parallel"],"verdict":"CONFIRMED"},"id":"e40fbe32c4f27f3c072493191","kind":"research.recorded","lamport":184708,"schema":1,"subject":"Rc5cf7f7395","ts":"2026-11-12T15:24:29.868493Z"}
